rpresser <rpresser@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Jul 2, 4:44 am, Patric Mueller <bh...@bigfoot.com> wrote:
>> Hi
>>
>> When being polymorphed into a vampire or demons, the player doesn't
>> write on the floor but scrawls:
>> "You scrawl on the floor with your fingers."
>>
>> The resulting message is "scrawled in blood".
>>
>> From reading the source (engrave.c:320) I know this is a reference to
>> Thriller.
>
> I don't really see why the "See you next Wednesday" comment is right
> there in the source. "See you next Wednesday" is one of the possible
> random inscriptions, and the comment next to it says Thriller there
> (engrave.c:25). But all sorts of things can be scrawled in blood.

I guess as "scrawled in blood" is not really a set phrase the
programmer thought it was necessary to add the reference.

Furthermore, the random message and "scrawled in blood" was added in
NetHack 3.1.0 but the message was already in garbled form:
nethack-3.1.0/src/engrave.c:26: "S?e you n?xt Wed?esd?y", /* Thriller */

Automatically wiping out of random engravings wasn't added until 3.2.0.

>> But even when writing on the floor with a soft object, the message is
>> still "scrawled in blood". Is this intentional?
>>
>> "You scrawl on the floor with a blindfold."
>> "What do you want to scrawl on the floor here?"
>> "You see a message scrawled in blood here."
>
> Well, from a source POV, the decision to scrawl in blood (type =
> ENGR_BLOOD) is made towards the beginning of doengrave()
>
> 	if (is_demon(youmonst.data) || youmonst.data->mlet == S_VAMPIRE)
> 	    type = ENGR_BLOOD;
>
> (Notice that it's not just vampires, but all demons, too.)

I didn't know that vampires are not demons in NetHack.

That makes Demonbane even less useful than I thought.

> This is
> then modified by the object used to engrave; hard rings, hard gems,
> sharp weapons end up engraving; boots always write in the dust; wands
> do all sorts of things. But if it doesn't get changed by any of that,
> it stays ENGR_BLOOD.

And this looks to me like an oversight.

Right now the default action is scrawling when one is a vampire or
demon regardless if one uses an object or not.

> Explaining this from a game POV? Well ... I guess it must be magic. :-)

Aaah, that explains everything. :-)

Okay one could claim that the pointy index fingers pokes through any
soft object and so one doesn't really write with the object but ...
that sounds a little bit far-fetched.

